IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF MATARAM CITY

NUMBER 4 OF 2012

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF MATARAM,

Considering:

a. that violence against women and children is an act that violates the rights and dignity of women and children as human beings;
b. that the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence has not been carried out optimally;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children from Acts of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 of 1993 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regional Municipality of Mataram (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1993 Number 66, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3531);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 Concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
6. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
10. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 Concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;
14. Regional Regulation of West Nusa Tenggara Province Number 2 of 2009 concerning the Implementation of Prevention and Protection of Women and Children Victims of Acts of Violence (Regional Gazette of West Nusa Tenggara Province of 2009 Number 15, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of West Nusa Tenggara Province Number 45);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MATARAM CITY

and

MAYOR OF MATARAM

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 3

1. The objectives of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence are to:
a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize harmonious and prosperous household integrity.
2. The objectives of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:
a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

Every victim has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal service;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and
g. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member and community member.

Article 5

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 4, also receive special rights, as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of regional government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government establishes action programs and activities for the protection of women and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for SKPD and UKPD in implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 15

Forms of services to victims organized by PPT, include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medicolegal services constitute a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services constitute services provided in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have self-confidence, strength and independence, in resolving their problems;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing legal processes; and
e. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.

Article 17

1. To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the regional government conducts empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 18

1. Prevention of the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 17 shall be implemented by SKPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD and UKPD as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 20

The provision of services and assistance to victims shall be conducted with the principles of:
a. fast, safe, and empathetic;
b. guarantee of confidentiality;
c. easily accessible; and
d. free of charge.

Article 21

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by SKPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
3. In implementing the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other regency/city governments, and the community.
4. Further provisions regarding procedures for services and handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 23

1. The Regional Government is obligated to conduct empowerment of women victims of acts of violence.
2. Empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by P2TP2A.
3. In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government prepares empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:
a. procuring the needs required for entrepreneurship training for women victims of acts of violence, in order to increase knowledge, attitude, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of women victims of acts of violence;
e. endeavoring to provide capital for women victims of acts of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting products of women victims of acts of violence.
4. In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 25

1. In order to achieve the objectives of providing protection to women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the regional government cooperates with:
a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments; and
d. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

CHAPTER XII

INVESTIGATION

Article 34

1. In addition to general investigation officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation, may also be conducted by civil servant investigators as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Code.
2. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are certain civil servant officials within the regional government appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. In carrying out investigation duties, investigation officials as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized to:
a. receive reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take first action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. seize objects and/or documents;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in expert witnesses needed in connection with case examination;
h. terminate the investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify this matter to the public prosecutor, suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
4. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the investigator official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.
